@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "2. Make the player move") {
        A player needs more than an appearance. In this lesson, give it velocity and write the rule that turns velocity into movement. Then let a tap reverse its direction.

        This builds on <doc:creating-your-first-scene>. Use the complete code stages to update `ContentView.swift`.

        @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-03-preview.svg", alt: "A preview of the scene you will build in this lesson.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Store speed in a component") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A component is a small piece of data. `MovementMotion` stores velocity: how many points the player should travel each second.

            A system contains a rule. `MovementSystem` asks the world for entities with that component, then moves each one. The same rule would work for one player or a whole group of moving objects.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Run stage one. The player travels from x = -100 to x = 100 and stops. Find the query for `MovementMotion`, then the line that adds `motion.velocity * dt` to `entity.position`.

                `deltaTime` is the time since the previous update, in seconds. Multiplying speed by time gives the distance to move this frame. This keeps movement measured in points per second rather than points per redraw.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-movement-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-01-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Change the velocity from 80 to 40 points per second. The trip should take about twice as long. The sprite component still controls appearance; the motion component now controls speed.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Give another system a different job") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Separate moving from deciding what happens at the edge. `MovementBoundsSystem` checks the new position, places the player back inside the limits, and reverses velocity.

            The order matters: move first, then check the boundary. Declaring `.after(MovementSystem.self)` states that relationship even if you add the systems in a different order.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use stage two. The player now patrols between the two limits. Read the bounds system's `dependencies`, then find where setup adds both systems to the world.

                When a system edits a copy of a component, it writes the changed value back with `components.set(motion)`.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-movement-02.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-movement-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-02-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Change the left and right limits in the bounds system. The player should turn at the new positions without any change to `MovementSystem`. You have two reusable rules instead of one rule that does everything.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Let the player respond to a tap") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Targeted input tells you which entity the user touched. `InputTargetComponent` makes the player eligible, and its collision rectangle supplies a hit area slightly larger than the visible square.

            The tap callback changes the motion component. On the next frame, the movement system uses that new velocity. The button below the scene separately enables or disables that system.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use the final stage. Tap the moving square to reverse it. Tap empty space and check that the counter does not change. Then try Pause movement and Resume movement.

                Follow the change from the gesture's `value.entity` to its motion component. The gesture chooses the entity; the system continues to own its ongoing movement.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-movement-03.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-movement-02.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-movement-03-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Pause movement and tap the stationary player. Its tap count can still change because disabling one system is different from pausing the whole world.

                You now know the main loop: components hold data, systems apply rules, and input changes data. Continue with <doc:controlling-a-player-with-input-actions> for held controls.
            }
        }
    }
}
